'''''Prunus spinosa''''', called '''blackthorn''' or '''sloe''', is a species of [[flowering plant]] in the rose family, [[Rosaceae]]. It is native to Europe and [[West Asia]], and has been [[naturalisation (biology)|naturalised]] in parts of [[North America]].

The fruit are used to make [[sloe gin]] in [[Great Britain]] and [[patxaran]] in the [[Basque Country (greater region)|Basque Country]]. The wood is used to make [[walking stick]]s, including the [[shillelagh (club)|Irish shillelagh]].

==Description==

''Prunus spinosa'' is a large [[deciduous]] [[shrub]] or small [[tree]] growing to {{convert|5|m}} tall, with blackish bark and dense, stiff, spiny branches. The [[leaves]] are oval, {{convert|2|–|4.5|cm|abbr=off|frac=8}} long and {{convert|1.2|–|2|cm|abbr=on|frac=8}} broad, with a serrated margin. The [[flower]]s are about {{cvt|1.5|cm|frac=4}} in diameter, with five creamy-white petals; they are produced shortly before the leaves in early spring,<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Clapham, A.C. |title=Excursion Flora of the British Isles |last2=Tutin, T.G. |last3=Warburg, E.F. |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=1968 |isbn=0-521-04656-4 |location=Cambridge, UK}}</ref> and are [[hermaphroditic]] and [[insect]]-pollinated. The [[fruit]], called a "sloe", is a single-seeded [[drupe]] {{convert|10|–|12|mm|frac=8}} in diameter, black with a purple-blue waxy bloom, ripening in late autumn. In the [[United Kingdom]], they are traditionally harvested in October or November, after the first frosts, as this makes the skin softer and easier to process for the purposes of making sloe gin.<ref name="Mabey-2007">{{cite book |last=Mabey |first=Richard |title=Food for Free |date=2007 |edition=3rd |location=London |publisher=HarperCollins Publishers |page=47}}</ref> Sloes are thin-fleshed, with a very strongly [[astringent]] flavour when fresh.<ref name="Rushforth 1999"/> Its fruit [[persistence (botany)|persists]] for an average of 36.7 days. The fruit averages 77.6% water, and their [[dry matter|dry weight]] includes 10.6% [[carbohydrate]]s and 0.6% [[lipid]]s.{{sfn|Ehrlén|Eriksson|1991}}

Blackthorn usually grows as a bush but can grow to become a tree to a height of {{cvt|6|m}}. Its branches usually grow forming a tangle.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kilbracken, J. |title=Larousse Easy Way Guide to Trees |publisher=Larousse |year=1995 |isbn=0-7523-0027X}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=''Prunus spinosa'' – Sloe, Blackthorn |url=https://pfaf.org/user/Plant.aspx?LatinName=Prunus+spinosa |website=PFAF Plant Database}}</ref>

''Prunus spinosa'' is frequently confused with the related ''[[Prunus cerasifera|P.&nbsp;cerasifera]]'' (cherry plum), particularly in early spring when the latter starts flowering a month or more earlier than ''P.&nbsp;spinosa''.<ref name="Rushforth 1999"/><ref name="Mitchell">{{cite book |last1=Mitchell |first1=Alan |last2=Dahlstrøm |first2=Preben |last3=Sunesen |first3=Ebbe |last4=Darter |first4=Christine |title=A field guide to the trees of Britain and Northern Europe |publisher=Collins |publication-place=London |date=1974 |isbn=0-00-212035-6 |page=291}}</ref> They can be distinguished by flower size, around 15&nbsp;mm ''P.&nbsp;spinosa'', and 20&nbsp;mm in ''P.&nbsp;cerasifera''. In addition, the [[sepals]] are bent backwards in ''P.&nbsp;cerasifera'', but not in ''P.&nbsp;spinosa''.<ref name="Tree Guide UK">{{Cite web |title=Blackthorn flowers |url=https://www.treeguideuk.co.uk/blackthorn-flowers/ |access-date=9 February 2022 |website=Tree Guide UK}}</ref> They can be distinguished in winter by the shrubbier habit with stiffer, wider-angled, grey-brown branches of ''P.&nbsp;spinosa'' (greenish, and flexible, in one-year-old shoots of ''P.&nbsp;cerasifera'');<ref name="Rushforth 1999"/> in summer by the relatively narrower leaves of ''P.&nbsp;spinosa'', more than twice as long as broad;<ref name="Rushforth 1999"/><ref name="Vedel-1978"/> and in autumn by the colour of the fruit skin purplish black in ''P.&nbsp;spinosa'' and yellow or red in ''P.&nbsp;cerasifera''; the latter also ripens about two months earlier, in late summer or early autumn.<ref name="Stace-2019">{{Cite book |last=Stace |first=C.A. |author-link=C. A. Stace |title=New Flora of the British Isles |publisher=C. & M. Floristics |year=2019 |isbn=978-1-5272-2630-2 |edition=4th |location=Middlewood Green, Suffolk, U.K.}}</ref>{{rp|207}}

''Prunus spinosa'' has a [[tetraploid]] (2''n''=4''x''=32) set of chromosomes.{{sfn|Weinberger|1975|pp=336–347}}

Like many other stone fruit with [[Endocarp|stones]], the seed in the stone contains trace amounts of [[hydrogen cyanide]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 November 2018 |title=Schlehen entkernen – ein Ding der Unmöglichkeit? |trans-title=Stoning sloes: An impossible thing? |url=https://www.gartenjournal.net/schlehen-entkernen |access-date=17 November 2021 |website=Garten Journal|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210613170048/https://www.gartenjournal.net/schlehen-entkernen |archive-date=13 June 2021 |language=de}}</ref>

