==Etymology==

The specific name {{lang|la|[[wikt:spinosa|spinosa]]}} is a Latin term indicating the pointed and thornlike [[spur shoot]]s characteristic of this species. The common name {{wt|en|blackthorn}} is due to the thorny nature of the shrub, and possibly its very dark bark; it has a much darker bark than the whitethorn ([[Common hawthorn|hawthorn]]), to which it is contrasted.<ref name="Johns-1882"/>

The word commonly used for the fruit, {{wt|en|sloe}}, comes from [[Old English]] {{lang|ang|[[wikt:slāh|slāh]]}}, [[cognate]] with [[Old High German]] {{lang|goh|[[wikt:sleha|slēha]]}}, {{lang|goh|[[wikt:slewa|slēwa]]}}, and Modern [[German language|German]] {{lang|de|[[wikt:schlehe|schlehe]]}}.<ref name="Whitney-1906"/> Other cognate forms are the Frisian and [[Middle Low German]]{{Efn|Both Frisian and Middle Low German were historically spoken in [[Lower Saxony]].}} {{lang|gml|slē}}, the [[Middle Dutch]] {{lang|dum|[[wikt:slee, slie, sleeu|slee, slie, sleeu]]}}; the [[Modern Dutch]] {{lang|nl|[[wikt:slee|slee]]}}; the Modern [[Low German]] {{lang|nds|[[wikt:slee|slee]]/[[wikt:slē|slē]]}}, {{lang|nds|[[wikt:sli|slī]]}};<ref name="Whitney-1906"/><ref name="OED-1933">{{Cite OED1|sloe}}</ref> and the [[Danish language|Danish]] {{lang|da|[[wikt:slåen|slåen]]}}.<ref name="Whitney-1906"/>

The names related to ''sloe'' come from the common [[Germanic languages|Germanic]] root {{wt|gem-pro|slaihwō}}, itself comparable to the [[Old Slavic language|Old Slavic]], Bulgarian, Macedonian, Ukrainian and Russian {{lang|ru|[[wikt:слива|слива]]}} ({{transliteration|ru|sliva}}, [[Ukrainian language|Ukrainian]] {{transliteration|uk|slyva}}),<ref name="OED-1933"/><ref name="Whitney-1906"/> and the [[West Slavic languages|West Slavic]]/[[Polish language|Polish]] {{lang|pl|[[wikt:śliwa|śliwa]]}}, referring to a plum of any species, including sloe. The root {{lang|pl|[[wikt:śliwa tarnina|śliwa tarnina]]}} is present in other [[Slavic languages]], such as [[Bosnian language|Bosnian]], [[Bulgarian language|Bulgarian]] {{lang|bg|[[wikt:трънка|трънка]]}}, [[Croatian language|Croatian]], [[Montenegrin language|Montenegrin]] and [[Serbian language|Serbian]] ({{lang|sh|[[wikt:šljiva|šljiva]]}}/{{lang|sh|[[wikt:шљива|шљива]]}}).

==Distribution and habitat==
''P. spinosa'' is native to Europe, western Asia, and locally in northwest Africa.<ref name="Rushforth 1999">{{harvnb|Rushforth|1999|p=227, 1031–1032 (also pages 1015–1017 for further details on comparison with ''P. cerasifera'')}}</ref><ref name="POWO">{{Cite web |title=''Prunus spinosa'' L. |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:730297-1 |access-date=21 March 2023 |website=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew}}</ref> It is also locally [[naturalisation (biology)|naturalised]] in [[Tasmania]] and eastern North America.<ref name="POWO"/>

==Ecology==
[[File:Taphrina pruni, Pocket Plum gall.JPG|thumb|Pocket plum gall on blackthorn, caused by the fungus ''[[Taphrina pruni]]'']]

The foliage is sometimes eaten by the [[larva]]e of [[Lepidoptera]], including the [[Eriogaster lanestris|small eggar moth]], [[Pavonia pavonia|emperor moth]], [[willow beauty]], white-pinion spotted, [[common emerald]], [[November moth]], [[pale November moth]], [[mottled pug]], [[green pug]], [[brimstone moth]], [[feathered thorn]], [[brown-tail]], [[yellow-tail]], [[short-cloaked moth]], lesser yellow underwing, [[lesser broad-bordered yellow underwing]], [[double square-spot]], [[black hairstreak]], [[brown hairstreak]], hawthorn moth (''[[Scythropia crataegella]]'') and the case-bearer moth ''[[Coleophora anatipennella]]''. Dead blackthorn wood provides food for the caterpillars of the [[concealer moth]] ''[[Esperia oliviella]]''.{{cn|date=August 2024}}

== Uses ==
The shrub, with its long, sharp thorns, is traditionally used in Britain and other parts of northern Europe to make a cattle-proof [[hedge]].{{sfn|Coats|1992|loc=Prunus}}

The fruit is similar to a small [[damson]] or [[plum]], suitable for preserves, but rather [[wikt:tart#English|tart]] and [[wikt:astringent#English|astringent]] for eating fresh unless it is picked after the first few days of autumn frost. This effect can be reproduced by freezing harvested sloes.<ref name="Brown-1994"/> This kind of ripening is called [[bletting]].

Since the plant is hardy, and grows in a wide range of conditions, it is used as a [[rootstock]] for many other species of plum, as well as some other fruit species.{{cn|date=August 2024}}

=== Flavouring ===
The juice was used in the manufacture of fake [[port wine]], and it was used as an [[adulterant]] to impart roughness to genuine port, into the early 20th century.<ref>{{Cite NIE|wstitle=Sloe|year=1905}}</ref><ref name="Rines-1920">{{cite Americana |wstitle=Sloe |year=1920 |inline=1}}</ref><ref name="White-1952"/> In rural Britain a [[liqueur]], [[sloe gin]], is made by infusing [[gin]] with sloes and sugar; [[vodka]] can also be infused with sloes.<ref name="Kerri-2010">{{Cite web |last=Kerri |date=10 Oct 2010 |title=Sloe gin and sloe chutney |url=http://dinnerdiary.org/2010/10/10/sloe-gin-and-sloe-chutney/ |access-date=31 August 2017 |website=Dinner Diary}}</ref> Similarly, in [[Northern Greece]], they make a blackthorn liqueur by infusing [[tsipouro]] with the fruit and adding sugar.{{cn|date=August 2024}}

In [[Navarre]], Spain, a popular liqueur called {{lang|eu|[[pacharán]]}} is made with sloes.{{cn|date=August 2024}} In France a liqueur called {{lang|fr|troussepinette}}, or just {{lang|fr|épine}} or {{lang|fr|épinette}}, is made from the young shoots in spring (rather than from the fruit in autumn).{{cn|date=August 2024}} ({{lang|fr|Vin d'épine}}, likewise, is an infusion of early shoots of blackthorn [[macerated]] with sugar in wine.)<ref name="Pasty-1999"/><ref name="Seaton-2017"/> In [[Italy]], the infusion of spirit with the fruit and sugar produces a liqueur called {{lang|it|[[bargnolino]]}} (sometimes {{lang|it|prunella}}).{{cn|date=August 2024}} In France, {{lang|fr|[[eau de vie]] de prunelle[s]}} is distilled from fermented sloes in regions such as the [[Alsace]]. Wine made from [[alcoholic fermentation|fermented]] sloes is made in Britain, and in [[Germany]] and other central European countries.{{cn|date=August 2024}} It is also sometimes used in the brewing of [[lambic]] beer in [[Belgium]].{{cn|date=August 2024}}

=== Culinary ===
Sloes can also be made into [[Fruit preserves|jam]], [[chutney]],<ref name="Kerri-2010"/> and used in fruit pies. Sloes preserved in [[vinegar]] are similar in taste to [[Japanese cuisine|Japanese]] {{transliteration|ja|[[umeboshi]]}}. The juice of the fruit dyes linen a reddish colour that washes out to a durable pale blue.{{sfn|Coats|1992|loc=Prunus}}

The leaves resemble tea leaves, and were used as an adulterant of tea.<ref name="Rines-1920"/><ref name="Beach-1914"/>

The [[endocarp|fruit stones]] have been found in Swiss lake dwellings.<ref name="Rines-1920" /> Early human use of sloes as food is evidenced in the case of the 5,300-year-old human [[mummy]] (nicknamed [[Ötzi]]), discovered in the [[Ötztal Alps]] along the Austrian-Italian border in 1991; a sloe was found near the remains, indicating that Ötzi intended to eat it before he died.<ref name="Ghose-2012">{{Cite web |last=Ghose |first=Tia |date=8 November 2012 |title=Mummy melodrama: Top 9&nbsp;secrets about Ötzi the Iceman |url=http://www.livescience.com/24666-otzi-iceman-mummy-life-death.html |access-date=10 November 2012 |website=LiveScience |location=part 7}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |year=2016 |title=Ötzi the Iceman |url=http://www.iceman.it/en/the-copper-age/ |access-date=2019-07-19 |series=The Copper Age |publisher=[[Museo Archeologico dell'Alto Adige]] |language=en}}</ref>

=== Wood ===
Blackthorn makes an excellent [[firewood]] that burns slowly with a good heat and little smoke.<ref>{{Cite report |url=http://www.scoutbase.org.uk/library/hqdocs/facts/pdfs/fs315001.pdf |title=The Burning Properties of Wood |publisher=[[The Scout Association]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121223202805/http://www.scoutbase.org.uk/library/hqdocs/facts/pdfs/fs315001.pdf |archive-date=2012-12-23 |year=1999}}</ref> The wood takes a fine polish and is used for tool handles and canes.<ref name="Beach-1914">{{cite NSRW |wstitle=Sloe |inline=1}}</ref> Straight blackthorn stems have traditionally been made into [[walking stick]]s or [[Club (weapon)|clubs]] (known in [[Ireland]] as a [[Shillelagh (club)|shillelagh]]).<ref name="Chouinard-2007">{{Cite report |url=http://www.cimande.com/blackthorn/pdf/stick_edited.pdf |title=The stick is king: The ''Shillelagh Bata'' or the rediscovery of a living Irish martial tradition <!-- http://www.freifechter.com/files/stick_edited.pdf --- dead 5 July 2011 --> |last=Chouinard |first=Maxime |access-date=17 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120101000000/http://www.freifechter.com/files/stick_edited.pdf |archive-date=1 January 2012 |url-status=live |year=2007}}</ref> In the British Army, blackthorn sticks are carried by commissioned officers of the [[Royal Irish Regiment (1992)|Royal Irish Regiment]]; this is a tradition also in Irish regiments in some [[Commonwealth of Nations|Commonwealth]] countries.{{cn|date=August 2024}}

=== Inks ===
[[Rashi]], a [[Talmudist]] and [[Tanakh]] commentator of the [[High Middle Ages]], writes that the sap (or [[Gum (botany)|gum]]) of ''P.&nbsp;spinosa'' (which he refers to as the {{lang|fr|[[:fr:Prunellier|prunellier]]}}) was used as an ingredient in the making of some [[Iron gall ink|inks used for manuscripts]].<ref>''[[Talmud|Talmud Bavli]], Tractate Shabbat 23a''</ref>

A "sloe-thorn worm" used as [[fishing bait]] is mentioned in the 15th-century work, ''[[Juliana Berners#Treatyse_of_Fysshynge_wyth_an_Angle|The Treatyse of Fishing with an Angle]]''.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Berners, Dame Juliana (attributed to) |author-link=Juliana Berners |url=http://www.farreaches.org/compendium/treatise/onetreatise.html |title=The Treatyse of Fishing with an Angle |date=3 August 2006 |publisher=Waking Lion Press |isbn=978-1-60096-446-6 |orig-date=c. 1420}}</ref>

== In culture ==

In the United Kingdom, the term '[[wikt:blackthorn winter|blackthorn winter]]' is an old phrase originating in rural England to describe a cold period in early Spring when blackthorn is in flower.<ref>[https://www.itv.com/news/2019-04-10/the-return-of-the-blackthorn-winter Verasamy, Lucy. "The return of the blackthorn winter", ITV News, 10 April 2019]</ref> 

The flowering of the blackthorn may have been associated with the ancient Celtic celebration of [[Imbolc]], traditionally celebrated on February 1 in Ireland, Scotland and the Isle of Man.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Aveni |first=Anthony F. |title=The Book of the Year: A brief history of our seasonal holidays |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |year=2004 |isbn=0-19-517154-3 |page=38}}</ref>

In [[Middle English]], {{lang|enm|slō}} was used to denote something of trifling value.<ref name="Lewis-1988">{{Cite encyclopedia |year=1988 |title=slō |encyclopedia=Middle English Dictionary |publisher=University of Michigan Press |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fLQc0_-iQiYC&pg=PA1063 |editor-last=Lewis |editor-first=Robert E. |page=1063 |isbn=0-472-01198-7 |via=Google Books}}</ref><ref name="OED-1933"/>

The expression "sloe-black eyes" for a person with dark eyes comes from the fruit,<ref>{{Cite book |last=James A. H. Murray |url=https://archive.org/details/oed9aarch/oed9aarch/page/n237/mode/2up |title=A New English Dictionary on Historical Principles: Founded Mainly on the Materials Collected by the Philological Society |date=1919 |publisher=Clarendon Press at Oxford |others=Kragen Javier Sitaker}}</ref> and is first attested in [[William Somervile]]'s 1735 poem ''The Chace''.<ref>{{Cite book |last=William Somerville (1675-1742) |url=https://archive.org/details/the-chase-1735/page/14/mode/2up |title=The chase; 1735 |date=1735}}</ref> {{wt|en|sloe-eyed|Sloe-eyed}}, meanwhile, is first attested in [[Augusta Jane Evans|A. J. Wilson]]'s 1867 novel ''Vashti''.<ref>{{OED|sloe-eyed}}</ref>

The name of the dark-coloured cloth [[Prunella (cloth)|prunella]] was derived from the French word {{Lang|fr|prunelle}}, meaning 'sloe'.<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |title=prunella |encyclopedia=Merriam-Webster |url=https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/prunella |access-date=2021-05-22 |via=merriam-webster.com}}</ref>

